-----------\nCello | Philharmonia (https://philharmonia.co.uk/resources/instruments/cello/)\nciteturn38view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://philharmonia.co.uk/resources/instruments/cello/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Search】\nL3: \nL4: Search\nL5: \nL6: Search the site [Input: Search]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Shop†shop.philharmonia.co.uk】 【3†Account†tickets.philharmonia.co.uk】 【4†Basket (0)†tickets.philharmonia.co.uk】 【5†Become a Friend】\nL9: \nL10: 【6†What's on】 Show Submenu for What's on\nL11: \nL12: 【6†What's on】\nL13: \nL14: Browse Philharmonia concerts and events in London, across the UK and internationally\nL15: 【7†2026/27 London season Concerts at the Southbank Centre's Royal Festival Hall】 【8†2026/27 Bedford season Concerts at our longest-standing residency】 【9†2026/27 Basingstoke season Orchestral favourites crown our season in Basingstoke】 【10†2026/27 Leicester season See some of classical music's biggest stars at De Montfort Hall】 【11†2026/27 Canterbury season Our home at the Marlowe Theatre】 【12†Free performances Our series of free, 45-minute pre-concert performances】\nL16: 【13†Learn & Take Part】 Show Submenu for Learn & Take Part\nL17: \nL18: 【13†Learn & Take Part】\nL19: \nL20: We inspire, excite, empower and engage a broad range of participants\nL21: 【14†Orchestra Unwrapped Our long-running programme of concerts for young people】 【15†Schools and Music Hubs We deliver projects and initiatives that support partners’ strategic aims】 【16†Talent Development We nurture and showcase the next generation of artistic talent】 【17†Artist in Residence Every season, we appoint an Artist in Residence to collaborate with our musicians.】 【18†Hear and Now Our award-winning community project uniting adults living with dementia with young people】 【19†Get closer to the music Insight talks, discussions and social events around our concerts.】\nL22: 【20†Our Story】 Show Submenu for Our Story\nL23: \nL24: 【20†Our Story】\nL25: \nL26: Founded in 1945, the Philharmonia is a world-class symphony orchestra for the 21st century\nL27: 【21†About us Read the Orchestra's current biography】 【22†Our players Meet our players, 80 outstanding musicians who come from all over the world】 【23†Our history This article, written to mark our 80th anniversary in 2025, reflects on the Philharmonia's history】 【24†Titled artists Meet the conductors and composers who shape our programme and performances】 【25†Digital & Immersive We use technology to connect with new audiences beyond the concert hall】 【26†Contact & Management All contacts for the Philharmonia's administration, and governance information】\nL28: 【27†Hire us】\nL29: \nL30: 【28†Support us】 Show Submenu for Support us\nL31: \nL32: 【28†Support us】\nL33: \nL34: As a registered charity, the Philharmonia relies on funding from a wide range of sources\nL35: 【5†Become a Friend Be part of an engaged community passionate about music and enjoy exclusive benefits】 【29†American Patrons The American Patrons of the Philharmonia Foundation support our work across the United States】 【30†Philharmonia in Bedford Trust Support us to secure the future of our residency at Bedford Corn Exchange in perpetuity】 【31†Philanthropy There are many ways that you can donate to help support the Philharmonia's programme】 【32†Corporate Partnerships We are proud to work in partnership with a wide range of businesses across the world】 【33†Thank you to our supporters We are grateful to our Philharmonia family of supporters whose contributions help us make extraordinary music accessible to everyone】\nL36: 【34†Menu】\nL37: # Cello\nL38: \nL39: The word cello is actually just an abbreviation of violoncello, an Italian word meaning “little violone”.\nL40: \nL41: 【35†Image: Philharmonia Orchestra, cello†philharmonia-assets.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com】\nL42: \nL43: 【36†Instrument: Cello†www.youtube.com】\nL44: \nL45: Video\nL46: ### Instrument: Cello\nL47: \nL48: No 2 Cello Karen Stephenson introduces her instrument.\nL49: \nL50: The Principal Cello Chair is endowed in memory of Amaryllis (1925-1999) by the Amaryllis Fleming Foundation and Fleming Family and Partners Ltd.\nL51: \nL52: The No. 2 Cello Chair is endowed by Jane and Julian Langer.\nL53: \nL54: The No. 3 Cello Chair is endowed by Gillian and Lionel Frumkin.\nL55: \nL56: Tutti Cello Chairs are endowed by:\nL57: Lia Larson & Ed Russel Ponte\nL58: Saul Nathan\nL59: Manuela Ribadeneira\nL60: Endowment opportunities at the Philharmonia offer supporters unique access and insights to our players. Find out more here:\nL61: \nL62: 【37†Find out more】\nL63: #### Introduction\nL64: \nL65: A violone was an instrument common in the 17th and 18th centuries which evolved into today’s double bass. The cello is the tenor voice in the string section. It can play an octave lower than the viola and, as with the other Strings, the cello section sits two to a desk. There are usually between eight and twelve cellos in a symphony orchestra.\nL66: Perhaps more than any other instrument the cello sound can create a melancholy mood. Its deep tenor voice can be further enhanced by a broad vibrato which on other instruments might sound ridiculous. With its particularly wide range and powerful sound it can one minute be playing a bass line and the next a melody that is high enough for the rest of the orchestra to move underneath.\nL67: Another of its useful qualities is its clarity of attack, which enables it to play crisp architectural shapes as well as driving rhythmic patterns and makes the cello a key component of the orchestra’s ‘engine room’.\nL68: #### Explore\nL69: \nL70: Why not download our iPad app, ‘The Orchestra’, to learn even more?\nL71: \nL72: 【38†Read more】\nL73: #### Range\nL74: \nL75: The Cello has four strings, tuned in 5ths to C, G, D and A. The lower strings have a richer, darker sound and to exploit this it is quite common for composers to write that a particular phrase should be played on a particular string. Writing Sul C (on the C) or Sul G above the music asks the player to continue playing on the low C or G string when they would otherwise have moved up onto one of the higher strings.\nL76: Most cello music will be written in the bass clef and occasionally moves into tenor clef as the music gets higher. When the music is very high the treble clef is used. Players do not like to change clefs too often and so will only want to go into a higher clef if the music is going to stay up high for a while. Changing back again presents similar problems and cello players will often mention that they do not like to read low notes in tenor clef.\nL77: 【39†Image: Cello range†philharmonia-assets.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com】\nL78: \nL79: Frequency Range\nL80: \nL81: 65 Hz – 1.0 kHz\nL82: \nL83: Strings\nL84: \nL85: C G D A\nL86: #### Bowing\nL87: \nL88: Notes that are indicated to be played separately can be played on the string or can allow the bow to bounce off the string. With a large body of string players, all playing repeated quavers together, for instance in the accompaniment of a piece by Haydn, Mozart or Beethoven, the effect of on the string versus off the string can be very different.\nL89: Bouncing off the string has more resonance and is probably easier for most orchestras to get together. Playing on the string is, on the contrary, more forceful, but is harder work and also harder to get together.\nL90: The style of letting the bow jump off the string was made popular by Mendelssohn in his “Midsummer Night’s Dream”. It allowed him to explore the character of the scherzo’s dancing fairies, which was difficult to express with the normal ‘on the string style’ of playing. Mendelssohn was one of the first people to make the ‘off the string’ style of playing popular in serious western classical music.\nL91: What flowed from that was more use of the bouncy stroke among players, particularly for accompaniment passages.\nL92: #### Did you know?\nL93: \nL94: Traditionally cellos are made of spruce and maple, but recently they’ve been brought bang-up-to-date with a carbon fiber version.\nL95: \nL96: ##\nL97: \nL98: More about the cello\nL99: \nL100: 【40†Cellist Steven Isserlis plays Kabalevsky encore #StayHome #WithMe†www.youtube.com】\nL101: \nL102: Video\nL103: ### Cellist Steven Isserlis plays Kabalevsky encore\nL104: \nL105: An intimate performance and a personal message from world-leading cellist Steven Isserlis\nL106: \nL107: 【41†Duet (violin & cello), STRAVINSKY Pulcinella Suite, Serenata #StayHome #WithMe†www.youtube.com】\nL108: \nL109: Video\nL110: ### Duet (violin & cello), Stravinsky Pulcinella Suite, Serenata\nL111: \nL112: Philharmonia cellist Ella Rundle and her husband Nathaniel Anderson-Frank perform one of the first pieces they ever played together\nL113: \nL114: 【42†Philharmonia Orchestra: Silent Night (Cello Octet)†www.youtube.com】\nL115: \nL116: Video\nL117: \nL118: ### Christmas Card 2016: Silent Night (Cello Octet)\nL119: \nL120: Enjoy our 2016 Christmas Card, filmed in St Mary de Castro, Leicester, with eight wonderful cellists\nL121: ## Join us on Instagram\nL122: \nL123: Keep up to date about online concerts, behind the scenes content and much more\nL124: \nL125: 【43†Follow us†www.instagram.com】\nL126: \nL127: 【44†Image†philharmonia-assets.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com】\nL128: \nL129: ## Keep up to date\nL130: \nL131: Sign up for email updates and be the first to receive stories, films and concert announcements\nL132: \nL133: 【45†Sign up†pages.wordfly.com】\nL134: \nL135: 【46†Image†philharmonia-assets.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com】\nL136: ## Support the Philharmonia\nL137: \nL138: Enjoyed this content? We need your help to keep these resources free\nL139: \nL140: 【47†Donate now†tickets.philharmonia.co.uk】\nL141: \nL142: 【48†Image†philharmonia-assets.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com】\nL143: \nL144: 【49†Image: Philharmonia logo】\nL145: \nL146: 【50†Image: Black lives 